# Cards A: the whole block, the profile, the horizons, the rock below, turf, litter, stones, how soil forms.
from soil_lib import *

CHILD = ['soil_profile', 'soil_geology', 'soil_turf', 'soil_flints', 'soil_crumbs', 'soil_coarse_root', 'soil_hyphae', 'soil_earthworm']

N('soil_block', 'The soil under your feet', 'habitat', 1.5, ['park_ground'],
  "A cut block of park soil 1.5 m across and 1.2 m deep, standing where the lawn meets an oak's roots: layers of soil and rock, stones, roots, fungal threads, crumbs, and the animals that live in it.",
  "Soil is the thin skin of the Earth where rock, air, water and living things meet. This block shows it as if the lawn had been cut open with a spade 1.2 metres deep. Under the grass comes a dark layer rich in dead plant material, then a darker, crumbly topsoil full of roots, then a paler subsoil, and at the bottom the pebbly sand that the soil grew from. Across all of it run oak roots, white fungal threads and the burrow of an earthworm, and in the surface layers live animals from a ground beetle to springtails smaller than a grain of rice. Choose a layer with the buttons: Surface, Topsoil, Subsoil, Whole profile cut, Life only (the soil turns see-through so only living things show) or Open the earthworm. Click anything to read about it, and zoom into the crumb or the clay to see what the soil is made of.",
  "Soil is a mixture of mineral grains (sand, silt and clay), dead and decaying organic matter, water, air and a very large number of living things. About half of a typical soil by volume is solid and about half is pore space, shared between water and air. The layers are called horizons, lettered O, A, B and C from the top down; this block draws all four. The soil of the Greenwich Park plateau forms in the Blackheath pebble beds, a layer of sand and rounded flint pebbles that lies at the top of the park. The block is a typical lawn soil, not a core from the park: the rock below is what the geological survey describes, the depths of the soil layers are typical values, and the animals, roots and stones are placed for illustration. Soils on these beds elsewhere south of London are mapped as acid, sandy soils, so the real soil is probably paler and sandier than a textbook loam.",
  "How the picture was made: every solid part is a closed mesh cut by a plane, so you see a true section of each horizon, stone, root and worm. Sizes are real where they are known and are stated on each card (the earthworm 170 mm by 8 mm, the woodlouse 15 mm, springtails 1.8 mm, oribatid mites 0.7 mm). Not to scale: fungal threads are drawn about 40 times thicker than the real 4 to 6 micrometre hyphae so that they can be seen, the density of grass blades, roots and crumbs is illustrative, and the colours on the cut face are a code. The park is itself a simplified scene, so this is not a core taken at a particular spot in Greenwich Park.",
  [('Block', '1.5 m across and 1.2 m deep; a slab 0.4 m thick behind the cut face'), ('Horizons drawn', 'O, A, B, C (typical depths)'), ('Rock below', 'Blackheath pebble beds (Harwich Formation)'), ('Animals', 'earthworm, woodlouse, ground beetle, springtails, oribatid mites'), ('Zoom-in scenes', 'a soil crumb; a clay plate with the ions it holds')],
  'C2', [R_SOIL, R_ECO], [GEOT, W_SOIL, W_HOR, LANDIS],
  parts=CHILD, related=['bio_soil_ecology', 'bio_plant_nutrition_soil', 'oak_roots', 'bio_primary_production_decomposition'],
  fig={'type': 'geometry', 'range': [0, 100, 0, 62], 'shapes': [
      {'k': 'rect', 'x': 5, 'y': 49, 'w': 58, 'h': 3, 'fill': '#3b2f20'}, {'k': 'rect', 'x': 5, 'y': 38, 'w': 58, 'h': 11, 'fill': '#4a3828'}, {'k': 'rect', 'x': 5, 'y': 16, 'w': 58, 'h': 22, 'fill': '#9a7b4e'}, {'k': 'rect', 'x': 5, 'y': 2, 'w': 58, 'h': 14, 'fill': '#cdb98c'},
      {'k': 'text', 'x': 66, 'y': 50.5, 'text': 'O  litter and thatch: about 3 cm', 'size': 11, 'align': 'left'}, {'k': 'text', 'x': 66, 'y': 43.5, 'text': 'A  topsoil: down to about 25 cm', 'size': 11, 'align': 'left'},
      {'k': 'text', 'x': 66, 'y': 27, 'text': 'B  subsoil: down to about 80 cm', 'size': 11, 'align': 'left'}, {'k': 'text', 'x': 66, 'y': 9, 'text': 'C  pebble bed: below', 'size': 11, 'align': 'left'}],
      'caption': 'The four horizons of the block drawn to scale (1.2 m deep). The depths are typical values chosen for the model; real soils vary from place to place.'})

N('soil_profile', 'The soil profile: horizons O, A, B and C', 'concept', 1.2, ['soil_block'],
  "A soil profile is the vertical section through a soil, from the surface down to the rock it grew from; its layers, called horizons, are lettered O, A, B and C.",
  "If you dig a pit, the soil is not the same all the way down. Soil scientists name the layers with letters. O is the surface layer made of dead plant material (leaves, grass thatch). A is the topsoil, where mineral grains are mixed with dark, decayed organic matter and where most roots and animals live. B is the subsoil, paler, with less living matter. C is the loose rock or sediment that the soil grew from, hardly changed. In the Whole profile cut view you can see all four with their typical depths.",
  "The letters follow the international descriptions of soil horizons: O is organic material at the top; A is a mineral layer at or near the surface where organic matter has been worked into the mineral grains; B is a mineral layer that formed below A and shows changes such as new structure or clay and iron brought down from above; C is unconsolidated mineral material with little or no soil formation in it. Horizons are roughly parallel to the surface and form in place as water, roots and animals work on the parent material. Their thickness varies from place to place; the depths drawn here (O about 3 cm, A to about 25 cm, B to about 80 cm) are typical values for a lawn soil, not measured at this spot. Darwin measured the dark mould that earthworms work as usually 4 or 5 to 10 and even 12 inches thick (about 10 to 30 cm), which is the range of the topsoil drawn here.",
  "Different classification systems (the World Reference Base, FAO, the US Soil Taxonomy) use the same master letters with different definitions, so horizon names are not interchangeable between them. Many soils have extra horizons: E, a pale layer from which clay and iron have been washed out, sits between A and B in some acid soils; R is hard rock. The profile drawn here is a simplified typical one for a lawn on sandy and pebbly parent material, not a described profile from a survey. On acid sandy parent material the pale E layer is common and humus and iron are washed down into the B layer: soils on Blackheath Beds south of London are mapped as humo-ferric podzols of this kind (LandIS). A clay-enriched B layer, as in many loams, would be less expected here. The wavy boundaries between the horizons are drawn to look natural, not measured.",
  [('O', 'organic: litter and thatch (drawn about 3 cm)'), ('A', 'topsoil: mineral grains with organic matter (to about 25 cm)'), ('B', 'subsoil: changed by weathering and by material moved down (to about 80 cm)'), ('C', 'parent material: little changed (below)'), ('Depths', 'typical values, not measured here')],
  'C1', [R_SOIL], [W_HOR, DARWIN, LANDIS], parts=['soil_horizon_o', 'soil_horizon_a', 'soil_horizon_b', 'soil_horizon_c'], related=['bio_plant_nutrition_soil'])

N('soil_horizon_o', 'O horizon: litter and thatch', 'structure', 0.03, ['soil_profile'],
  "The O horizon is the thin top layer of a soil made of dead plant material: leaf litter and the matted dead grass and roots of the lawn.",
  "On the lawn this layer is only a few centimetres thick: the felt of old grass stems and roots (called thatch) and, near the oak, a scatter of fallen leaves. It is the soil's larder and its first stage of recycling. Fungal threads grow through it, and woodlice, springtails and mites eat their way through it. Below it, the dark topsoil begins.",
  "The O horizon holds organic material that is only partly decayed; in some descriptions a 'litter layer' is the part where more than 90 % of the volume is still recognisable dead plant tissue. Much of what lies at the top of a lawn is thatch: grass leaves, stems and roots that die faster than they decompose. Darwin watched earthworms drag leaves into their burrows, which is one way plant remains are carried from the surface into the soil.",
  "The O horizon is drawn 3 cm thick, a model choice. The litter on the surface (oak leaves about 10 cm long, tilted into the grass) shows how the layer is fed from above. Fungal threads in the layer are drawn about 40 times too thick; real hyphae are 4 to 6 micrometres across. In Whole profile cut the layer shows a dark pattern; its colours, like all the cut-face colours, are a code.",
  [('Made of', 'dead plant material: litter, thatch, dead roots'), ('Drawn thickness', '≈ 3 cm (model choice)'), ('Eaten and mixed by', 'woodlice, springtails, mites, earthworms, fungi, bacteria')],
  'C1', [R_SOIL, R_FUNGI], [W_HOR], parts=['soil_litter', 'soil_hyphae', 'soil_springtail', 'soil_mite', 'soil_woodlouse'], related=['bio_primary_production_decomposition'])

N('soil_horizon_a', 'A horizon: the topsoil', 'structure', 0.22, ['soil_profile'],
  "The A horizon is the topsoil: mineral grains mixed with dark, decayed organic matter, where most roots, fungi and soil animals live.",
  "This is the layer you would call 'the soil' in a garden. It is dark because decayed plant material (humus) has been mixed into the mineral grains, mostly by earthworms and other animals. It is crumbly: the grains are stuck into little lumps called crumbs, with pores between them for air and water. Grass roots and the oak's fine roots spread through it, and most of the animals in this block live in it. In the model it reaches to about 25 cm.",
  "Because organic matter, roots and animals are concentrated here, the A horizon is the most biologically active part of the profile. Darwin found that the dark vegetable mould of old pasture was usually 4 or 5 to 10, and even 12, inches thick, and that earthworms pass it over and over through their bodies. In the block the topsoil shows the pocket of crumbs and pores on the cut face, flint pebbles, fungal threads and fine roots with their fungal sleeves, and the burrow of the earthworm.",
  "The boundary with the O horizon above is drawn at 3 cm and with the B horizon below at about 25 cm, with a gentle undulation; both depths are typical values, not measured. The pocket of crumbs (4 to 9 mm across) is drawn on a patch of the face about 36 cm wide; in real topsoil crumb structure continues through the layer, and crumb size and stability depend on the soil.",
  [('Made of', 'mineral grains + humus + living things'), ('Drawn depth', '≈ 3 to 25 cm (typical)'), ('Structure', 'crumbs (aggregates) with pores between them'), ('Darwin', 'worm-worked mould usually 4 or 5 to 10 or 12 inches thick (≈ 10 to 30 cm)')],
  'C1', [R_SOIL], [W_HOR, DARWIN, W_STRUCT], parts=['soil_crumbs', 'soil_fine_roots', 'soil_earthworm', 'soil_pores'], related=['bio_soil_ecology', 'bio_plant_nutrition_soil'])

N('soil_horizon_b', 'B horizon: the subsoil', 'structure', 0.55, ['soil_profile'],
  "The B horizon is the subsoil: a paler, firmer layer below the topsoil that weathering has changed. In many soils it collects clay or iron washed down from above; on acid sandy gravels it is more likely to be sandy.",
  "Under the dark topsoil the soil turns paler and yellower-brown. There is much less dead plant matter here, fewer roots and far fewer animals. In many soils this is the zone where water seeping down leaves behind fine clay particles and iron compounds. The block is drawn as a typical lawn soil, so it includes a little clay here; the soils on the Blackheath sands and gravels are probably sandier. In the block, a thick oak root crosses this layer and its cross-section shows in the cut. Click the clay hotspot to look at a single clay particle.",
  "The B horizon forms below the A horizon and shows soil-forming changes: new structure, new clay formed by weathering, or material moved down from above (a process called illuviation). On very acid sandy material the moved material is mostly humus and iron rather than clay, giving a podzol; the soil association that LandIS maps over the Blackheath and Woolwich Beds south of London is dominated by such humo-ferric podzols. A few earthworm burrows from the deep-burrowing species go through the B horizon and let roots follow them down.",
  "The B horizon is drawn between about 25 and 80 cm, a typical depth, not a measured one. No soil survey of Greenwich Park itself was found, so whether the soil at any spot is podzolic, a brown earth or something disturbed by park use is not known; the horizon is drawn as a generic subsoil. Texture and colour depend on the exact site.",
  [('Made of', 'mineral grains, some clay, iron compounds; little organic matter'), ('Drawn depth', '≈ 25 to 80 cm (typical)'), ('Living things', 'few; deep roots and the burrows of deep-burrowing earthworms'), ('On acid sands', 'sandier and less clay-rich than a loam')],
  'C2', [R_SOIL], [W_HOR, LANDIS], parts=['soil_clay', 'soil_coarse_root', 'soil_flints'], related=['bio_plant_nutrition_soil'])

N('soil_horizon_c', 'C horizon: the pebble bed the soil grew from', 'structure', 0.4, ['soil_profile', 'soil_geology'],
  "The C horizon is the loose rock that a soil forms in. Here it is drawn as the Blackheath pebble bed: pale sand with many rounded flint pebbles.",
  "At the bottom of the block lies the material the soil above has been made from. It is almost unchanged: no dark organic matter, few roots, no animals. Under Greenwich Park's high plateau this material is sand with many rounded black pebbles, all of them flint. The pebbles do not weather quickly, so they stay behind as the sand around them becomes soil, and some of them reach the surface.",
  "In soil science, C is unconsolidated mineral material (you could dig it with a spade) in which soil formation has had little effect. Beneath it, the geological column continues, here for several metres, as the next card describes. The pebbles in this layer are drawn 2.4 to 7 cm across; their number is illustrative.",
  "The block ends at 1.2 m, well inside a bed that the geological survey puts at up to 10 m thick at the top of the park, so the real C horizon continues far below the bottom of the block. The pebble sizes and number are model choices; what is sourced is that the pebbles are rounded flint and that the sand and gravel are what lies below the plateau.",
  [('Parent material (model)', 'Blackheath pebble bed: sand and rounded flint pebbles'), ('Bed thickness at the top of the park', 'up to about 10 m (London Geodiversity Partnership)'), ('Pebbles', 'rounded flint (model: 2.4 to 7 cm)')],
  'C2', [R_SOIL], [GEOT, W_HOR], parts=['soil_flints', 'soil_geology'], related=['boulder', 'rock_grains'])

N('soil_geology', 'What lies under Greenwich Park', 'concept', 10, ['soil_block', 'soil_horizon_c'],
  "The high plateau of Greenwich Park stands on the Blackheath pebble beds, a gravel and sand formed in a shallow sea about 55 million years ago, and below it lie clays and sands, then Thanet Sand and Chalk.",
  "Greenwich Park is a hill: its steep slope falls about 25 metres to the River Thames, and the flat top, the Blackheath plateau, is made of sand and gravel. The pebbles in the gravel are rounded black flints, which started as layers in the Chalk, were washed out and rolled for a long time, probably by the sea, and were laid down again. Water falling on the plateau soaks down through the gravel and sand until it meets clay about 15 metres below the top of the slope, and comes out as springs. The block you see is a very thin slice of the top of that story: soil on the sand and pebbles.",
  "From the top down, as the London Geodiversity Partnership's geotrail describes it: the Blackheath pebble beds (the Blackheath Member of the Harwich Formation), up to about 10 m thick on Blackheath and at the top of the park; then the sands and clays of the Lambeth Group (including the Woolwich and Reading formations and, at the base, the Upnor Formation sand); then the Thanet Sand; then the Chalk. A spring line appears where the permeable gravel meets clay. The Harwich Formation dates from about 55 million years ago, and the Blackheath pebbles were laid down under the sea. The top surface of the pebble bed is at much the same height, 40 to 45 m, from Blackheath to Erith, 13 km away.",
  "Younger deposits lie elsewhere: the Thames flood plain at the foot of the hill is alluvium (clays, sands and pebbles deposited in the last 10,000 years) and the Kempton Park Gravel, a Thames terrace laid down 100,000 to 50,000 years ago, is near the surface a few steps from the river. The block shows only the soil on the plateau's pebble bed. The geotrail does not describe the soil itself; the soil layers in the block and their depths are typical for a lawn soil on sand and gravel, not read from a survey of the park. The park register describes the southern half as high gravel plateau and the northern part as low-lying flood plain, separated by a ridge about 25 m high. Chalk lies deep below the park (it was mined nearby, in caverns at Blackheath), and at 1.2 m the block is nowhere near it.",
  [('Plateau rock', 'Blackheath pebble beds (Harwich Formation): sand and rounded flint pebbles, up to about 10 m'), ('Age', 'about 55 million years (Harwich Formation); laid down under the sea'), ('Plateau height', '40 to 45 m above sea level; the slope falls about 25 m to the Thames'), ('Below', 'Lambeth Group clays and sands, Thanet Sand, Chalk'), ('Park area', '≈ 74 hectares')],
  'C1', [R_SOIL], [GEOT, PARKSGARD], parts=['soil_horizon_c', 'soil_flints'], related=['boulder', 'rock_grains'])

N('soil_turf', 'Lawn grass and its root mat', 'organism', 0.04, ['soil_block'],
  "A lawn is a dense mat of grass leaves above and a felt of fine roots and dead stems below, which holds the soil together and feeds the layers underneath.",
  "The lawn here is drawn 4 centimetres tall, and what you see is only the upper part of each plant. Each plant is a tuft of narrow leaves that grow from the base, so mowing does not kill them. Below the surface the same plants have a dense mass of fine roots in the top layer of soil. Together, the leaves and the root mat form the turf. The roots hold the soil, the dead leaves and roots become the thatch, and each time the grass is cut the clippings add dead plant matter that decomposers recycle. In the model, one patch of lawn covers the top of the block and sways a little in a drawn breeze.",
  "Grasses are flowering plants whose leaves grow from the base, which is why they survive grazing and mowing. Their roots are fibrous: many fine roots rather than one tap root. A mat of such roots binds the soil particles; fine roots release sugars and other compounds that feed bacteria and fungi around them (the rhizosphere), and root and fungal products help stick mineral grains into crumbs. Like other plants, grass absorbs most of its water and nutrients through its fine roots and root hairs.",
  "The block draws 5,000 blades, each 4 cm high, as five-triangle strips in tufts; the number is illustrative and real lawns have far more. The species mix is not modelled (a typical British lawn contains several grasses and often white clover, a nitrogen-fixing legume; see the nitrogen card). Cellulose, the main structural material of the blades, can be viewed as a molecule fragment (cellotriose).",
  [('Mown height', '≈ 4 cm (drawn)'), ('Blades in the model', '5,000 (illustrative)'), ('Made of', 'cellulose-rich cell walls'), ('Roots', 'fibrous, mostly in the topsoil')],
  'C1', [R_ROOT, R_SOIL], [W_STRUCT], parts=['soil_fine_roots'], related=['rn_cellulose', 'bio_roots_mycorrhiza'])

N('soil_litter', 'Leaf litter', 'structure', 0.1, ['soil_horizon_o'],
  "Fallen oak leaves lying on the lawn edge: the first food of the soil's recycling system.",
  "Each autumn an oak drops its leaves. They lie on the ground and are the raw material of the O horizon. Fungi and bacteria soften them, woodlice and springtails and mites chew them, and earthworms pull pieces down into their burrows and eat them. Over time the leaf is eaten, digested, mixed with mineral grains and turned into the dark organic matter of the topsoil. The leaf shown is about the size of a real oak leaf, 10 centimetres long.",
  "Leaf litter is mostly cellulose and lignin, the same materials as wood, plus smaller amounts of sugars, proteins and minerals. Soft parts go first; tough, lignin-rich parts last. Decomposition is the work of many organisms in sequence, not of one. Mixing litter into the mineral soil protects some of its carbon from further decay.",
  "The oak leaf in the card is drawn from a lobed outline 8 to 12 cm long (the oak card gives 10 to 12 cm); 46 leaves are scattered with a lean to the oak side. How fast a leaf disappears depends on climate, the leaf and the animals present; the model gives no rate. Cellulose can be viewed as a molecule.",
  [('Leaf size', '8 to 12 cm (oak leaves are 10 to 12 cm)'), ('Mostly made of', 'cellulose and lignin'), ('Eaten by', 'fungi, bacteria, woodlice, springtails, mites, earthworms')],
  'C1', [R_FUNGI, R_CYC], [W_WOOD, DARWIN], parts=['soil_decomposition'], related=['oak_leaf', 'oak_lignin_cellulose', 'bio_primary_production_decomposition'])

N('soil_flints', 'Flint pebbles', 'structure', 0.04, ['soil_horizon_c', 'soil_geology'],
  "Flint pebbles are the rounded, dark, extremely hard stones in the Blackheath pebble bed: lumps of quartz that formed in chalk and were rolled smooth.",
  "The stones in this block are flints. Flint is a very hard stone made of quartz (silica) in crystals so small you cannot see them, which formed as lumps inside the Chalk, long before the sand above it. When the Chalk wore away, the flints were washed out and tumbled until they were rounded, and were laid down again in the Blackheath pebble bed. Break one and the edge is sharp, which is why flint was used for tools. In the block they are thickest in the bottom layer; a few have worked up into the soil and one or two lie on the lawn.",
  "Flint is a cryptocrystalline form of quartz (silicon dioxide, SiO2), found chiefly as nodules in chalk and limestone, with a dark grey or black interior and usually a white rough rind. The geotrail says the Blackheath pebbles are all flint that originated as layers within the White Chalk, that they were rounded by long transport, probably by the sea, and that the size of the pebbles implies deposition by high-energy currents. Because flint is so resistant to weathering, it stays as the softer minerals around it break down. Quartz can be viewed as a crystal.",
  "Pebble sizes in the block (2.4 to 7 cm) and their number are model choices; what is known is that the pebbles are rounded flint. In the cut, each pebble shows a dark cross-section. The quartz crystal you can open from this card is a lattice of alpha quartz, the mineral flint is made of, not the microcrystalline texture of flint itself.",
  [('Material', 'cryptocrystalline quartz (SiO2)'), ('Origin', 'nodules in the White Chalk'), ('Shape', 'rounded by transport; sizes in the block 2.4 to 7 cm (model)'), ('Weathering', 'very resistant')],
  'C1', [R_SOIL], [GEOT, W_FLINT], parts=[], related=['boulder', 'rock_grains'])

N('soil_formation', 'How soil forms, and how long it takes', 'process', 1, ['soil_block'],
  "Soil forms slowly, as rock breaks down and living things add organic matter. Where it has been measured, the fastest rates of turning rock into soil range from about 0.05 to 2 millimetres a year, so a few centimetres take centuries.",
  "Soil is made from rock by weathering: rain, frost and chemistry break rock into smaller pieces, and plants, fungi, bacteria and animals mix in dead matter. Time matters most. It takes decades to several thousand years for a soil to develop a profile with layers. Where rates have been measured they are small: at some sites around a tenth of a millimetre of rock becomes soil each year, so a centimetre of soil can take about a hundred years. Earthworms speed up the mixing in a different way: Darwin buried chalk on a field and found it seven inches deep 29 years later, because worm casts piled up on top of it.",
  "Five things shape a soil, the classical factors named by Jenny in 1941: the parent material, the climate, the topography, the organisms and the time. Rates of soil production depend on how thick the soil already is: the soil production function says the rate falls roughly exponentially as the soil thickens, from a maximum P0 that was reported to range from 0.08 to 2.0 mm a year for sites in northern California and 0.05 to 0.14 mm a year for sites in south-east Australia. In Sicily a production rate of approximately one tenth of a millimetre a year was reported. These are rates of turning rock into soil, a different process from the burial of objects by worm casts, and they say nothing about this particular block.",
  "No measured rate of soil formation for Greenwich Park is used here. Chemical weathering roughly doubles for each 10 degrees C rise in temperature but depends strongly on water. At 0.1 mm a year, 10 mm of soil takes 100 years, and a soil 25 cm deep would need about 2,500 years. The topsoil of old pasture can also thicken from above, because earthworms bring fine earth up: Darwin's measurement is on the Darwin card. The rates quoted here are summaries of published field studies at particular sites; they are not typical of every soil.",
  [('Maximum rate of rock to soil, reported sites', '≈ 0.05 to 2 mm a year'), ('Sicily example', '≈ 0.1 mm a year'), ('Time to develop a profile', 'decades to several thousand years'), ('Five factors', 'parent material, climate, topography, organisms, time')],
  'C2', [R_SOIL], [W_FORM, W_SPF, DARWIN], parts=['soil_darwin', 'soil_geology'], related=['bio_plant_nutrition_soil'],
  fig=spans([('S.E. Australia (several sites)', .05, .14), ('N. California (several sites)', .08, 2.0), ('Sicily (one report)', .1, .1)], 'mm a year', 'Reported maximum rates of turning rock into soil, each bar spanning the range found across sites (log scale, 0.01 to 10 millimetres a year). A bar is the spread between places, not an uncertainty. Darwin\'s worm-cast burial, about 5.6 mm a year, is a different process and is left out.', .01, 10, ' mm', True))

